 [m
[31m-## Purpose[m
[31m-[m
[31m-This document defines a reproducible and leakage-aware evaluation plan for the portfolio optimizer used by **AXIOM Portfolio Intelligence**.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-It intentionally does **not** publish unverified performance numbers. Results such as CAGR, Sharpe ratio, drawdown, or benchmark outperformance must only be added after they are generated by committed code, stored as versioned artifacts, and independently reproducible.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-> Historical results are not forecasts. AXIOM is an educational and research system, not an investment-advice or trade-execution service.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Evaluation questions[m
[31m-[m
[31m-The backtest should answer:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-1. Does the optimizer outperform an equal-weight portfolio after costs?[m
[31m-2. Does it improve risk-adjusted performance relative to an appropriate market benchmark?[m
[31m-3. Does the FinBERT sentiment overlay add measurable value over quantitative optimization alone?[m
[31m-4. Are allocations stable, diversified, and practical to rebalance?[m
[31m-5. Does performance remain positive across different market regimes and parameter choices?[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Required strategies[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Evaluate at least these strategies on identical dates and assets:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-| Strategy | Description |[m
[31m-| --- | --- |[m
[31m-| Buy and hold | Initial allocation held without scheduled rebalancing |[m
[31m-| Equal weight | Assets receive equal weights at every rebalance |[m
[31m-| Quantitative only | MPT/risk optimizer without sentiment influence |[m
[31m-| Sentiment only | Bounded FinBERT signal without MPT allocation |[m
[31m-| AXIOM combined | Adaptive quantitative and sentiment pipeline |[m
[31m-| Market benchmark | Suitable index for the selected asset universe |[m
[31m-[m
[31m-RAG/LLM text should not directly change historical portfolio weights unless its decision rule is deterministic, versioned, and reproducible. The LLM is primarily an explanation layer in the current product.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Point-in-time correctness[m
[31m-[m
[31m-A valid financial backtest must only use information available at the simulated decision time.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Required controls:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-- use adjusted price data available before each rebalance[m
[31m-- use historical index constituents rather than today’s constituents when possible[m
[31m-- respect article publication timestamps[m
[31m-- do not fetch current news to simulate past sentiment[m
[31m-- fit return, covariance, and calibration parameters only on the training/lookback window[m
[31m-- execute simulated trades after the signal becomes available[m
[31m-- record data revisions and provider limitations[m
[31m-[m
[31m-If point-in-time news is unavailable, run and label the test as **price-only**. Do not invent historical sentiment values.[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Suggested walk-forward design[m
[31m-[m
[31m-| Setting | Recommended starting value |[m
[31m-| --- | --- |[m
[31m-| Price lookback | 252 trading days |[m
[31m-| Rebalance frequency | Monthly |[m
[31m-| Minimum history | 126–252 observations per asset |[m
[31m-| Risk-free rate | Explicit annual assumption stored with each run |[m
[31m-| Maximum asset weight | 20–30%, tested through sensitivity analysis |[m
[31m-| Transaction costs | Explicit basis-point cost per traded notional |[m
[31m-| Slippage | Explicit and separate from commission assumptions |[m
[31m-| Benchmark | Equal weight plus market index matched to the universe |[m
[31m-[m
[31m-At each rebalance date:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-1. construct the eligible universe using information available at that date[m
[31m-2. select the historical lookback window[m
[31m-3. estimate expected returns and covariance[m
[31m-4. calculate quantitative candidate weights[m
[31m-5. compute point-in-time sentiment only when valid news data exists[m
[31m-6. select the adaptive blend and constraints without using future returns[m
[31m-7. apply turnover and concentration rules[m
[31m-8. deduct costs from executed weight changes[m
[31m-9. hold the portfolio until the next rebalance[m
[31m-10. store inputs, outputs, model versions, and performance[m
[31m-[m
[31m-## Core calculations[m
[31m-[m
[31m-For prices `P` and daily returns `r`:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```text[m
[31m-r[t] = P[t] / P[t-1] - 1[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-For portfolio weights `w`, expected returns `mu`, and covariance matrix `Sigma`:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```text[m
[31m-Expected return = w.T @ mu[m
[31m-Variance        = w.T @ Sigma @ w[m
[31m-Volatility      = sqrt(variance)[m
[31m-Sharpe ratio    = (annual return - risk-free rate) / annual volatility[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Turnover at rebalance `t`:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```text[m
[31m-turnover[t] = sum(abs(target_weight - pre_trade_weight))[m
[31m-```[m
[31m-[m
[31m-Net return must deduct modeled trading costs:[m
[31m-[m
[31m-```text[m
[31m-net_return[t] = gross_return[t] - turnover[t] * cost_rate[m
[31m-```[m
